{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"S2 (e042) ReconcilerSettings captures `comment_max_chars` and `jira_cli_timeout` at compose, but neither is threaded into the built backend: the DC sanitizer's `comment_max_chars()` still calls `resolve_comment_max_chars()` ambiently on first use, and the Cloud ACLI transport's per-call subprocess timeout still resolves `reconciler.jira_cli_timeout` ambiently inside `_run_acli` (`_acli_call_timeout()`). Complete the operation-scoped capture for these two fields. Advisory finding from Gerrit 1843 PS1 LLM-Review.\n\n## Approach\n\n- `JiraDataCenterBackend.__init__` (src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py): when a captured `scope` is present, construct `self.sanitizer = _DCSanitizer(comment_max_chars=scope.comment_max_chars)` via the existing injection seam; `scope is None` keeps the legacy lazy ambient resolve. `<= 0` already means UNLIMITED in `_truncate_dc_comment_body`, matching `resolve_dc_comment_max_chars`'s 0 convention, so the captured value threads verbatim.\n- `AcliClient` (src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli.py): accept keyword `call_timeout: int = 0` and forward it from `_run` to `_run_acli`.\n- `_run_acli` (src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli_subprocess.py): gain `call_timeout: int | None = None` — a positive value bounds `communicate(timeout=...)`; non-positive/`None` falls back to the existing ambient `_acli_call_timeout()`, so direct constructions and legacy build paths stay byte-identical.\n- `ReconcilerRuntime._build_cloud` (src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/runtime.py): pass `call_timeout=s.jira_cli_timeout` when building the real `AcliClient`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A DC backend built with a captured scope truncates comments at the scope's `comment_max_chars` without consulting ambient config — RED-first test monkeypatches `resolve_comment_max_chars` to return a different value and asserts the scope value wins; verified by `python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_operation_bindings_runtime.py -q`.\n- [ ] A DC backend built WITHOUT a scope keeps the legacy lazy ambient resolve — existing bug-049e coverage stays green; verified by `python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler -k \"dc or datacenter\" -q`.\n- [ ] `AcliClient(call_timeout=N)` forwards N to `_run_acli` and bounds the subprocess wait at N seconds, while an unset/0 `call_timeout` falls back to `_acli_call_timeout()` — pinned by a unit test; verified by `python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_operation_bindings_runtime.py tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_acli_subprocess_timeout.py -q`.\n- [ ] `ReconcilerRuntime._build_cloud` threads the captured `jira_cli_timeout` into the constructed `AcliClient` — asserted in the runtime bindings test; verified by `python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_operation_bindings_runtime.py -q`.\n- [ ] Gates pass: `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` all exit 0.\n\n## Testing\n\nRED-first: add failing tests in `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_operation_bindings_runtime.py` (scope-threaded DC sanitizer ceiling; `AcliClient` `call_timeout` forwarding; `_build_cloud` wiring), confirm they fail, then implement until green. Regression: `python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler -q`, plus `make lint` and `make typecheck`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787437975659057001,"uuid":"19b2ece5-b6e2-42cc-9aeb-ff515b9ce6df"}